.TH MAGUS-VCS 1 "" "magus" "magus Manual"
.SH NAME
magus\-vcs \- Staging and conflict resolution that knows what is generated
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B magus
.RI "vcs <add|resolve|checkpoint|merge\-driver> [flags]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Version\-control operations that read the workspace's output declarations,
so generated files and hand\-written sources are treated differently.
.PP
add stages what the workspace declares: sources, and the generated outputs a
source change in the same commit accounts for. Anything undeclared is reported
rather than swept in, which is the difference between it and git add \-A.
.PP
\-\-untracked says yes to every undeclared path at once, so it needs \-\-reason: it
drops the one report that separates this command from git add \-A, and the files
it sweeps in land in a commit everybody pulls. The reason is kept with the
staging verdict. Naming a path stages one file without the flag.
.PP
resolve settles an in\-progress merge, rebase, or cherry\-pick. It classifies
every conflicted path at once, regenerates once instead of once per file,
settles the files one side deleted (which no VCS invokes a merge driver for),
and stages everything the regeneration touched so a following commit or
rebase \-\-continue does not refuse on a dirty tree. Conflicts in files magus
does not generate are reported and left alone. Pass \-\-against <ref> to merge
that ref first and settle what it conflicts with; add \-\-dry\-run to see the
classification and have the merge backed out again.
.PP
merge\-driver is the per\-file driver git and hg invoke during a merge. You do
not run it by hand; it is wired per clone, because a driver registration
cannot be committed. That is also why a forge reports conflicts your own
clone would settle silently, and why resolve exists as the bulk counterpart.
.PP
checkpoint prints the identity of the working state right now \- head revision,
branch, whether the tree is dirty, and a digest of the uncommitted patch. Record
one when you hand a piece of work out, so a later reader knows what that work was
looking at. It RESOLVES AND RECORDS and never MINTS: no tag, no stash, no ref, no
file, nothing changed anywhere, so a checkpoint nobody keeps has cost nothing.
Feed the revision to anything that takes one; compare two digests to learn whether
two workers saw the same uncommitted tree, which the revision alone cannot say.
.PP
resolve works on git, Mercurial and Jujutsu. Only \-\-against is git\-only: merge the
base in yourself on the others, then run resolve.
.SS vcs add options
.RS
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fIstring\fR
Why the undeclared files belong in this change; required with \-\-untracked, kept with the staging verdict
.TP
\fB\-\-untracked\fR
Also stage undeclared files; requires \-\-reason
.RE
.SS vcs resolve options
.RS
.TP
\fB\-\-against\fR \fIref\fR
Merge this `ref` first, then settle what it conflicts with
.RE
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.RS
.TP
\fBadd\fR
Stage a change the way this workspace's declarations say it should be staged
.TP
\fBresolve\fR
Settle an in\-progress merge's conflicted generated files, then regenerate once
.TP
\fBcheckpoint\fR
Print the working state's identity, for recording what a lease was handed; writes nothing
.TP
\fBmerge\-driver\fR
The per\-file merge driver git and hg invoke; you do not run this by hand
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
\fIStage a change without sweeping in build residue\fR
.PP
.EX
magus vcs add
.EE
.PP
\fIClassify the dirty tree, stage nothing\fR
.PP
.EX
magus vcs add --dry-run
.EE
.PP
\fISweep in the undeclared files, on the record\fR
.PP
.EX
magus vcs add --untracked --reason "new fixtures the go spell does not claim"
.EE
.PP
\fISettle a conflicted merge\fR
.PP
.EX
magus vcs resolve
.EE
.PP
\fIMerge the base in and settle it in one step\fR
.PP
.EX
magus vcs resolve --against origin/main
.EE
.PP
\fIRecord what a lease was handed\fR
.PP
.EX
magus vcs checkpoint
.EE
.PP
\fIThe one citable token, for a ledger cell\fR
.PP
.EX
magus vcs checkpoint -o name
.EE
.PP
